  "note": "Every keyboard command on the \"Keyboard & Mouse Commands\" page of the Paint.NET documentation (revision date 3 August 2024). paint.net is Windows only, so each action carries a Windows chord and nothing else. The context is the page's own section heading (UI / Canvas Controls, Image List and Image Navigation, Tool Bar, the seven menus, the Settings dialog, the four dockable windows, Tool Shortcuts, and then one section per tool), which is why the same command appears under both Layers Menu and Layers Window and why the tool letters appear both under Tool Shortcuts and under the tool's own section - that is how the page lists them. Rows the page documents with a set of arrow keys (Shift current tool per pixel and 10 pixels, Move selection, Move Line/Curve, Move Shape) become one action per arrow with the chord in parentheses, and rows with several alternative chords (Cut, Copy, Paste, Close Image, Next/Previous image, Deselect, Commit text or tool drawing to layer, Scroll left/right/up/down, Exit Paint.NET) become one action per chord, also with the chord in parentheses. Repeat presses that the page marks \"x 2\", \"x 3\" or \"(twice)\" are written as a sequence of the same chord, as the page's own preamble defines them (\"the number of times the key should be pressed\"); where the page offers both a repeat-press form and a single chord for the same command the single chord is kept, and where it offers two repeat-press forms the shorter one is kept - so Rectangle Select is S, Magic Wand is Shift+S, Lasso Select is S then S and Ellipse Select is Shift+S then Shift+S. Left out because the chord grammar cannot express them: every mouse row (click, drag, the mouse wheel, the four-arrow reposition icon), the Spacebar-held pan and zoom rows, \"Show Menu Hotkey\" and \"Use Menu Hotkey\" which are the bare Alt key and Alt plus an underlined letter, \"Brush Width presets via Arrow Keys\" which needs the toolbar value box clicked first, and \"Switch to image number X\", where X is a placeholder for the image number rather than the letter X. The combined \"Zoom in / out\" row in UI / Canvas Controls is left out as well: it bundles the mouse wheel, Ctrl and the -/+ keys, Ctrl+0 and the status-bar slider into one cell, and the View Menu section already lists Zoom In, Zoom Out and Actual Size separately. The Diagnostic Aid's tilde is written as the grave key, which is the same physical key the page's own note describes on a US standard keyboard. The Command Line section is a shell invocation, not a shortcut.",
